lisa-design:
- Round 2 (design §13: R62, R63, R72). Base unchanged (main @ 181097e, no merge needed).
- R62 listing JSON uses `treeDigest` (camelCase), as design §13 R62 writes it and as the artifact routes already do (`mediaType`, `sizeBytes`); the round-2 prompt's `tree-digest` was not used. Files are `{path, sha256 (plain hex, like PolicyFileReport), size, package?}`; single file `{path, package?, sha256, source}`. Bodies are bare objects (no `data` envelope), like POST /agent/artifacts.
- R62 file routes: ETag = artifact.Digest(response body), not a digest-derived constant, so a later API version that renders the same artifact differently gets a new ETag even though Cache-Control is immutable. If-None-Match (weak compare, lists, *) -> 304. The raw GET route is unchanged except that its digest param is unescaped like the new routes (pathParam: Echo leaves params escaped when the request has a RawPath, e.g. lib%2Fhelpers.rego).
- R62 single file over 1 MiB or not valid UTF-8 -> 422 (the spec gives the cap, not the code; 413 is for request bodies). `/files/` with an empty path is served as the listing by the server's trailing-slash handling; GetFile still guards an empty path with 400.
- R62 package: artifact.ModulePackage parses with the OPA AST as Rego v1, then v0, so v0 vendor modules still get a package (the agent's own PolicyFileReport uses v1 only).
- R62 PUT config-report rejects a malformed artifact-digest (bundle or extends) with 400, like effective-digest; existence is not checked.
- R63 shared rules: the risk template service's limits, hint vocabulary and template label-key extraction moved to dependency-free pkg/risktemplate (the agent imports pkg/policyeval and must not pull gorm). A parity test pins risktemplate.ValidHint to the service's own hint check. riskrel.RiskLevel is untouched.
- R63 Issue/codes are policyeval.Issue{File,Row,Col,Package,Severity,Code,Message}; severities are the defaults from the R63 table and callers re-weigh them. Static and dynamic layers share the Go-value validators (checkRiskTemplate, violationProblems); static converts literals with an "unknown" sentinel for calls/refs/unbound vars/comprehensions, resolving a var through a top-level `v := X`/`v = X` in the rule body or a single unconditional constant rule of the package.
- R63 severity choices: no `violation` rule is a WARNING everywhere (design table), including regocheck; only a missing title is escalated to ERROR for self-contained authored bundles. The round-2 prompt said "missing title/violation -> error otherwise"; design §13 lists no-violation as a warning, so the design wins (a constant-satisfied policy is valid, just suspicious).
- R63 regocheck downgrades missing-title to a warning when the bundle extends a source OR is a patch of a bundle some validation base's file defines (regocheck.WithPartialBundles, passed by validateCandidate from fileBundleNames(bases)). Standalone saves (no bases) can't know about file bundles, so an overlay patching a file bundle without a title in that package is blocked there.
- R63 risk_templates must be an array: a set literal, set comprehension or `risk_templates contains` is an error, even though OPA returns sets as []any and the agent would accept them (R63 lists multi-value contract keys as errors).
- R63 violation shapes: `violation contains X` / v0 `violation[X]` and v1 `violation[X] if` (partial object with value true; Execute reads the keys) are accepted; `violation[k] := v` with a non-true value is invalid-violation-rule (the values are ignored); complete set/array/comprehension values are accepted, complete scalars/objects are errors. Duplicate risk template names are errors in both layers (the agent seeds template UUIDs from name+package+file, so the batch upsert would reject a duplicate id); names are compared within one array literal, not across else branches.
- R63 Execute: Result.Issues = ValidateResult(result) for every result; Execute's error semantics are unchanged. Playback: top-level `issues` (CheckContract on the request modules parsed as v1) and `results[].issues`; both always [] when empty; neither fails the request.
- PolicyError gains `code` (omitempty): policyeval.Issue* codes for contract problems and regocheck.Code{Parse,MissingRegoV1,PackageNamespace,ForbiddenBuiltin} for the parse-level checks. Bundle-shape PolicyErrors keep an empty code.
- Agent impact: regocheck.ValidatePolicyBundles (called by agent prepareInline on merged bundles) now includes the contract layer, so the agent gets the static R63 check by bumping the pin; it should not run CheckContract again on the same authored modules.

